      "title": "Hess 与 Peterson 2015，PDF 第 8 页 Data Analysis",
      "uri": "doi:10.1371/journal.pone.0136973",
      "snapshotAttachmentId": "e9e284779f2d91dd0d6792a2a687e3c688fb40fce124f2959bed9902fa880438",
      "locator": "第 8 页",
      "excerpt": "We obtained 1,978 responses to our questionnaire (Table 1) and excluded 154 from our analy-\nsis, because the respondents identified themselves as living outside the United States. We ana-\nlyzed the remaining 1,824 responses"
    },
    {
      "id": "article-scope",
      "title": "Hess 与 Peterson 2015，PDF 第 1 页 Abstract",
      "uri": "doi:10.1371/journal.pone.0136973",
      "snapshotAttachmentId": "e9e284779f2d91dd0d6792a2a687e3c688fb40fce124f2959bed9902fa880438",
      "locator": "第 1 页",
      "excerpt": "We administered a web-based survey, using Twitter for recruitment, to\nexamine how well three US traffic control devices communicated the message that bicy-\nclists are permitted in the center of the travel lane and do not have to “get out of the way” to\nallow motorists to pass without changing lanes: “Bicycles May Use Full Lane” and “Share\nthe Road” signage, and Shared Lane Markings on the pavement. Each was compared to an\nunsigned roadway. We also asked respondents whether it was safe for a bicyclist to occupy\nthe center of the travel lane. “Bicycles May Use Full Lane” signage was the most consis-\ntently comprehended device for communicating the message that bicyclists may occupy\nthe travel lane and also increased perceptions of safety."
    },
